.* BOOKIE SCRIPT -- BOOKIE: BookMaster Markup on VM/370plus (VMP-0004)
.* User's guide and reference, and how bookie is built.  Format with
.*   BOOKIE BOOKIE (PS        on CMS, or
.*   bookie.py --groff ps -o bookie.ps BOOKIE.SCRIPT
:userdoc.
:prolog.
:prodname.VM/370plus
:subtitle.Virtual Machine Facility/370, ESA/390 Edition
:title.BOOKIE: BookMaster Markup on VM/370plus
:stitle.VM/370plus BOOKIE User's Guide and Reference
:version.Release 1 &bullet. Kit Build 16, 14 October 2026
:based.based on VM/370 Community Edition V1 R1.2
:docnum.VMP-0004-0
:filenum.S370-34
:eprolog.
:frontm.
:tipage.
:edition title='First Edition (October 2026)'.
:p.This edition applies to bookie as shipped with VM/370plus kit build
16 (milestones 9.30 and 9.31) and to all subsequent builds until
otherwise indicated in new editions.
:p.BookMaster is a licensed program of International Business Machines
Corporation. bookie is not BookMaster and contains no part of it: it is
an independent implementation of the BookMaster markup language,
written for the VM/370plus project from the published description of
that language. VM/370plus is a hobbyist project; this book is not a
publication of IBM.
:p.groff is GNU troff, Ghostscript is from Artifex; both are free
software and run in the Debian guest of VM/370plus.
:eedition.
:toc.
:h1.Preface
:p.This book tells you how to write documents in BookMaster markup and
format them with BOOKIE on VM/370plus, and how bookie itself works. It
is written for CMS users who know a text editor and the CMS file
system. If you have written BookMaster or Waterloo SCRIPT/GML documents
before, chapters 3 to 7 will be familiar and chapter 9 lists what is
different.
:p.The book is itself a BookMaster document. Its source is BOOKIE
SCRIPT, and it was formatted by bookie.
:dl tsize=14.
:dthd.Chapter
:ddhd.Contents
:dt.1
:dd.What BOOKIE is and what it produces.
:dt.2
:dd.A first document, the BOOKIE command, and printing.
:dt.3 to 7
:dd.The markup: document structure, paragraphs, lists, examples,
figures, tables, references, symbols and control words.
:dt.8
:dd.How bookie works: the path from CMS to Debian and back, the
translator, the vmplus style and the formatting passes.
:dt.9
:dd.Differences from IBM BookMaster.
:dt.A, B
:dd.Tag and symbol summaries.
:edl.
:body.
:h1.Introduction
:p.BookMaster was IBM's markup language and formatter for manuals. A
BookMaster document is plain text with :hp1.tags:ehp1.: a colon, a
name, optional attributes and a period. &colon.h1. starts a chapter,
&colon.p. a paragraph, &colon.ul. a list. The author marks what each
piece of text is; the formatter decides how it looks.
:p.BookMaster is licensed and does not exist for VM/370.
:hp2.bookie:ehp2. is the VM/370plus implementation of its markup.
You write a BookMaster
source file on CMS and get back:
:ul.
:li.a plain text document (fn DOCUMENT), for the terminal or a line
printer;
:li.PostScript (fn PS), for a PostScript printer, for PSPRINT, or for a
PDF.
:eul.
:p.The default style, :hp2.vmplus:ehp2., gives the look of the
VM/370plus manuals: A4 pages, a title page and edition notice in the
IBM manner, a table of contents with page numbers, chapters headed
:q.Chapter n.:eq. under a heavy rule, a running head and foot,
examples in a grey box, ruled tables and numbered figures. The book
you are reading shows that style. A second style, :hp2.ms:ehp2., gives
the plain look of the troff ms macros.
:p.bookie does its formatting in the Debian guest, with groff. CMS
users do not see that: BOOKIE EXEC sends the document through the
spool bridge and receives the result in their reader.
:h1.Getting Started
:h2.What you need
:ul.
:li.The Debian guest running (in MAINT: CP IPL LINUX), with the
vmspoold daemon, which starts with it.
:li.BOOKIE EXEC, on MAINT's 19D disk (and on the language disk).
:li.For PostScript output, a CMS disk with room for it: a 15-page book
is about 3,000 lines of 80 columns.
:eul.
:h2.A first document
:p.Create HELLO SCRIPT with XEDIT:
:xmp.
:userdoc.
:prolog.
:title.My First Book
:docnum.TEST-0001-0
:eprolog.
:frontm.
:tipage.
:toc.
:body.
:h1.Hello
:p.This is a paragraph with a :hp2.bold:ehp2. word.
:ul.
:li.one item,
:li.and another.
:eul.
&colon.euserdoc.
:exmp.
:figcap.A first BookMaster document
:p.Then format it:
:xmp.
bookie hello
BOOKIE: HELLO DOCUMENT A, 198 lines
Ready;
bookie hello (ps
BOOKIE: HELLO PS A, nnn lines
Ready;
:exmp.
:figcap.Formatting as text and as PostScript
:p.The text comes back as HELLO DOCUMENT A. With the PS option the
PostScript comes back as HELLO PS A, and a copy stays in the Debian
guest in /var/spool/vmspool/out/:hp1.userid:ehp1./hello.ps.
:h2.The BOOKIE command
:xmp.
BOOKIE fn [ft [fm]] [(PS]
:exmp.
:dl tsize=8.
:dt.fn
:dd.The file name of the source.
:dt.ft
:dd.Its file type; the default is SCRIPT.
:dt.fm
:dd.Its file mode; the default is *, the first disk that has it.
:dt.PS
:dd.Make PostScript (fn PS A) instead of text (fn DOCUMENT A).
:edl.
:p.Messages from the formatter (for example groff warnings) are shown
on the terminal. The return code is 0 when the document was made, 28
when the source was not found, and the service's return code when the
formatting failed.
:h2.Printing and PDF
:p.PSPRINT prints a PostScript file:
:xmp.
PSPRINT fn [ft [fm]] [(BRIDGE [format]]
:exmp.
:ul.
:li.Without BRIDGE the file goes to the virtual printer 00E without
page headers. Hercules writes CP's printer to a file on the PC, and
tools/psprint.py takes the PostScript out of it and prints it or, with
--pdf, makes a PDF.
:li.With BRIDGE the file goes to the Debian guest, where Ghostscript
turns it into the printer's format (ps, pdf, ljet4, pxlmono or
pxlcolor) and sends it to the printer named in
/etc/vmspool.d/print.conf.
:eul.
:h2.Formatting on Linux
:p.In the Debian guest, or on any machine with Python 3 and groff,
bookie.py runs directly:
:xmp.
bookie.py x.script                       the troff, on stdout
bookie.py --groff text x.script          formatted text
bookie.py --groff ps -o f.ps x.script PostScript
bookie.py --style ms ...                 the plain ms style
bookie.py -I dir ...                     where .im files are found
ps2pdf f.ps f.pdf                        a PDF
:exmp.
:figcap.bookie.py options
:h1.Document Structure
:h2.The parts of a book
:p.A complete document has this shape:
:xmp.
:userdoc.               the document starts
:prolog.                fields for the title page
  ...
:eprolog.
:frontm.                front matter
:tipage.                the title page
:edition title='...'.   the edition notice ...
:eedition.
:toc.                   the table of contents
:h1.Preface             front-matter headings
:body.                  the chapters
:h1.First Chapter
  ...
:appendix.              appendixes
:h1.Tag Summary
:backm.                 back matter
&colon.euserdoc.              the document ends
:exmp.
:figcap.The parts of a document
:p.Only &colon.body. and the text are needed. A memo can be a few
headings and paragraphs; bookie then gives pages without a title page
and contents.
:h2.The prolog
:p.The tags in the prolog collect the fields of the title page. Each
takes the rest of its line:
:fig.
:table cols='9 22'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Tag
:c.On the title page (vmplus style)
:ethd.
:row.
:c.&colon.prodname.
:c.The product name, large, at the top
:row.
:c.&colon.subtitle.
:c.The line under the product name
:row.
:c.&colon.title.
:c.The title of the book
:row.
:c.&colon.stitle.
:c.The short title: the running head of every page
:row.
:c.&colon.version.
:c.The release or version line (or &colon.date.)
:row.
:c.&colon.based.
:c.A second, smaller line (or &colon.author.)
:row.
:c.&colon.docnum.
:c.The publication number: on the title page and in every running
foot
:row.
:c.&colon.filenum.
:c.The file number, under the publication number
:etable.
:figcap.Prolog tags
:efig.
:p.&colon.tipage. prints the title page from these fields. Put it in
the front matter, after &colon.eprolog. and &colon.frontm..
:h2.Edition notice, preface and contents
:p.&colon.edition title='...'. starts the edition notice on a page of
its own, under the title you give; &colon.eedition. ends it. Its
paragraphs are ordinary &colon.p. paragraphs.
:p.Headings in the front matter (before &colon.body.) are front-matter
headings: a new page, no chapter number, not in the contents.
&colon.preface. and &colon.abstract. start such a section with that
name.
:p.&colon.toc. places the table of contents. It lists chapters and
their first-level sections (&colon.h1. and &colon.h2.) with dotted
leaders and page numbers.
:h2.Headings
:p.&colon.h0. to &colon.h6. are headings. The heading text is the
rest of the line. In the body:
:dl tsize=10.
:dt.&colon.h1.
:dd.A chapter: a new page, :q.Chapter n.:eq. and the title, a heavy
rule. &colon.h0. is treated as &colon.h1..
:dt.&colon.h2.
:dd.A section, in 13-point Helvetica bold; in the contents.
:dt.&colon.h3.
:dd.A subsection, in 11-point Helvetica bold. &colon.h4. to &colon.h6.
look the same.
:edl.
:p.A heading may carry an identifier, &colon.h2 id=setup., for
references (chapter 7). A heading is never left alone at the foot of a
page.
:p.After &colon.appendix. the chapters are appendixes.
:h1.Paragraphs and Highlighting
:h2.Paragraphs
:dl tsize=12.
:dt.&colon.p.
:dd.A paragraph. Text is filled and justified; line ends in the source
do not matter.
:dt.&colon.lp. &colon.pc.
:dd.A list paragraph and a paragraph continuation; formatted like
&colon.p..
:dt.&colon.note.
:dd.A paragraph that begins with a bold :q.Note&colon.:eq..
:dt.&colon.attention.
:dd.A paragraph that begins with a bold :q.Attention&colon.:eq..
:edl.
:note.This is what &colon.note. gives.
:h2.Highlighting
:p.Highlighting phrases are written inside a line and must be ended:
:fig.
:table cols='9 9 13'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Start
:c.End
:c.Result
:ethd.
:row.
:c.&colon.hp0.
:c.&colon.ehp0.
:c.Roman (normal)
:row.
:c.&colon.hp1.
:c.&colon.ehp1.
:c.:hp1.Italic:ehp1.
:row.
:c.&colon.hp2.
:c.&colon.ehp2.
:c.:hp2.Bold:ehp2.
:row.
:c.&colon.hp3.
:c.&colon.ehp3.
:c.:hp3.Bold italic:ehp3.
:row.
:c.&colon.hp4. to &colon.hp9.
:c.&colon.ehp4. to &colon.ehp9.
:c.As 1 to 3 again
:row.
:c.&colon.q.
:c.&colon.eq.
:c.:q.Quoted:eq.
:row.
:c.&colon.cit.
:c.&colon.ecit.
:c.:cit.A citation:ecit. (italic)
:row.
:c.&colon.xph.
:c.&colon.exph.
:c.:xph.Example phrase:exph. (Courier)
:etable.
:figcap.Highlighting
:efig.
:p.&colon.fn. ... &colon.efn. is a footnote. In the vmplus style it is
printed in parentheses where it stands; in the ms style it goes to the
foot of the page.
:h1.Lists
:h2.Ordered, unordered and simple lists
:p.&colon.ol. makes a numbered list, &colon.ul. a list with bullets,
&colon.sl. a list without marks. Each item starts with &colon.li.; the
list ends with &colon.eol., &colon.eul. or &colon.esl.. Lists nest: a
list inside an item is indented, and a nested unordered list uses
dashes.
:xmp.
:ol.
:li.Log on to MAINT.
:li.Start Linux:
:ul.
:li.from its disk: CP IPL LINUX, or
:li.from the reader: LINUX.
:eul.
:li.Format the document.
:eol.
:exmp.
:figcap.A nested list
:p.gives:
:ol.
:li.Log on to MAINT.
:li.Start Linux:
:ul.
:li.from its disk: CP IPL LINUX, or
:li.from the reader: LINUX.
:eul.
:li.Format the document.
:eol.
:h2.Definition lists
:p.&colon.dl. starts a definition list. tsize= sets the width of the
term column, in characters (default 12). Each entry is a term,
&colon.dt., and its description, &colon.dd.. &colon.dthd. and
&colon.ddhd. give a heading row. &colon.edl. ends the list. A term
longer than the column gets its description on the next line.
:xmp.
:dl tsize=8.
:dthd.Term
:ddhd.Meaning
:dt.CP
:dd.The Control Program.
:dt.CMS
:dd.The Conversational Monitor System.
:edl.
:exmp.
:figcap.A definition list
:p.A glossary list, &colon.gl. with &colon.gt. and &colon.gd. and
&colon.egl., is formatted the same way.
:h1.Examples, Figures and Tables
:h2.Examples
:p.&colon.xmp. ... &colon.exmp. is an example: a screen, a program, a
command and its answer. Lines are printed as they are, in Courier, in
a light-grey framed box, without filling. Tags inside an example are
not interpreted, so you can show markup; symbols (chapter 7) are.
&colon.lines. ... &colon.elines. keeps line ends too, in the body
font, without the box: for addresses and verse.
:attention.Control words are still recognized inside an example. A
line that starts with a period and an asterisk is a comment and is
dropped, as in BookMaster.
:h2.Figures
:p.&colon.fig. ... &colon.efig. is a figure: usually an example or a
table, and a caption. &colon.figcap. is the caption: the rest of the
line, printed as :q.Figure n.:eq. and the text. Figures are numbered
through the book.
:h2.Tables
:p.A table is a set of rows of cells:
:xmp.
:table cols='6 20'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Command
:c.What it does
:ethd.
:row.
:c.BOOKIE
:c.Formats a BookMaster document.
:row.
:c.PSPRINT
:c.Prints PostScript.
:etable.
:exmp.
:figcap.A table
:p.cols= gives the relative widths of the columns; the table is as
wide as the page. A cell is everything after &colon.c. up to the next
&colon.c., &colon.row. or &colon.etable., over as many lines as you
like, with highlighting. The rows between &colon.thd. and &colon.ethd.
are the heading, in Helvetica bold; a table that runs over a page
break repeats its heading on the next page. Every cell is ruled.
:h1.References, Symbols and Control Words
:h2.References
:p.&colon.hdref refid=xxx. is replaced by the text of the heading that
has id=xxx; &colon.figref. works the same way. The heading must come
before the reference in the source (see chapter 9).
:h2.Symbols
:p.A symbol is an ampersand, a name and a period. It stands for a
character that is hard to type or that would otherwise be markup.
&amp.colon. gives a colon, so &amp.colon.p. shows the tag &colon.p.
without starting a paragraph; &amp.amp. gives an ampersand. Appendix B
lists them all.
:p.&colon.nameit symbol=vm text='VM/370plus'. defines your own:
afterwards &amp.vm. gives the text. A symbol that is not known is
printed as written.
:h2.Control words
:p.Lines that start with a period and two letters are SCRIPT control
words. bookie knows these:
:dl tsize=10.
:dt.&period.*
:dd.A comment: the line is ignored.
:dt.&period.sp [n]
:dd.Space: n blank lines (default 1).
:dt.&period.br
:dd.Break: start a new line.
:dt.&period.pa
:dd.Page: start a new page.
:dt.&period.ce [n]
:dd.Center the next n lines (default 1).
:dt.&period.im name
:dd.Imbed: read the file name (on CMS: name with the same file type)
in place of this line.
:edl.
:p.Other control words are ignored.
:h1.How bookie Works
:p.bookie is three pieces of code, about 1,300 lines together: the
CMS front end, the translator and the style. groff does the
typesetting.
:fig.
:xmp.
CMS                                 Debian guest
---                                 ------------
BOOKIE EXEC
  .im files imbedded
  BOOKIE CARDS A
  LXCALL BOOKIE TEXT|PS fn
       -- punch: "bookie FROM user" -->  vmspoold
                                         /etc/vmspool.d/bookie
                                         bookie.py --groff text|ps
                                           BookMaster -> troff
                                           groff -mvmplus -t
                                           (again, until the
                                            page numbers stand)
       <-- punch: VMSPOOL REPLY ------   text, or messages,
                                         ::PS and PostScript
  LXCALL RESULT A
fn DOCUMENT A  or  fn PS A
:exmp.
:figcap.The path of a document
:efig.
:h2.The CMS side: BOOKIE EXEC
:p.BOOKIE EXEC (REXX) reads the source with EXECIO. A line that starts
with &period.im is replaced by the named file (same file type, any
disk), one level deep. The result is written to BOOKIE CARDS A.
:p.It then calls LXCALL, the general client of the spool bridge, with
the service bookie and the argument TEXT, or PS and the file name.
LXCALL's own options are passed in LXCALL OPTIONS A (FILE BOOKIE CARDS
A NOTYPE) rather than on the command line, because of an old
limitation with long argument lists (I-277).
:p.LXCALL punches the request to the Debian guest, waits for the reply
in the reader and keeps it as LXCALL RESULT A. BOOKIE EXEC copies the
answer into fn DOCUMENT A, or, for PostScript, shows the messages up
to the line ::PS and writes what follows to fn PS A.
:h2.The bridge: vmspoold
:p.vmspoold is a C daemon in the Debian guest. It reads the guest's
virtual reader through the vmur driver. A punch file whose first card
is :q.service FROM userid [arguments]:eq. is a request: vmspoold runs
/etc/vmspool.d/service with the arguments, feeds the other cards to
its standard input, and punches the output back to the user as
VMSPOOL REPLY, with VMSPOOL RC n as the first card. A handler that
runs longer than the daemon's limit is stopped: 900 seconds as the
Debian guest starts it (vmspoold -t 900).
:p.The bookie handler is a short shell script. For TEXT it runs
bookie.py --groff text. For PS it checks the name, writes the
PostScript to /var/spool/vmspool/out/user/name.ps, reports its size
and page count, and then sends ::PS and the PostScript.
:h2.The translator: bookie.py
:p.bookie.py is a Python 3 program. It reads the source line by line
(Latin-1; a CMS file arrives as EBCDIC and is converted by the
bridge):
:ol.
:li.Control words are handled first; &period.im reads the imbedded
file, up to 8 levels.
:li.In an example, the line is copied as it is, with symbols replaced.
:li.Otherwise the line is scanned for tags. A tag is a colon, a name,
attributes (name=value, quoted or not) and a period. Names that bookie
does not know are left in the text.
:li.:hp2.Block tags:ehp2. (headings, paragraphs, lists, examples,
tables) end the text so far and become troff requests.
:hp2.Inline tags:ehp2. (highlighting, quotes, references) become
troff escapes inside the text.
:li.Text is passed on with troff's special characters escaped; a text
line that would start with a period is protected.
:eol.
:p.The translator keeps a little state: the prolog fields being
collected, the stack of open lists, the current table (rows and cells
until &colon.etable.), the heading ids for references, the names of
&colon.nameit. symbols.
:p.Two classes produce the output. Bookie, the original, emits the ms
macros (.NH, .PP, .IP, .DS, .TS) of groff's ms package. Vmplus, a
subclass, emits only the BK- macros of vmplus.tmac: .BK-TITLE,
.BK-EDITION, .BK-CONTENTS, .BK-H, .BK-P, .BK-LIST, .BK-LI, .BK-DT,
.BK-XMP, .BK-FIGCAP, .BK-TS and so on. Tables become tbl input in
both: allbox, one format line per row, every cell in a T{ T} text
block, and in vmplus a .TS H heading that repeats on each page.
:h2.The style: vmplus.tmac
:p.vmplus.tmac is a groff macro package of about 500 lines. It defines
the page (A4, 2 cm margins, 17 cm lines, Times 10.5 on 13 points),
the running head and foot (traps at the top and at 27.4 cm), the
title page and edition notice, the contents page, chapter and section
headings, paragraphs, notes, the four kinds of list, the example box,
captions and the table hooks. The same macros work under nroff, so the
text output has the same structure with character spacing.
:p.Headings report themselves to bookie: each one writes a line
:q.BKTOC level page text:eq. to standard error with groff's .tm
request.
:h2.Page numbers in the contents
:p.The contents come before the chapters, but their page numbers are
known only after the chapters are formatted. bookie.py --groff
therefore formats the document, collects the BKTOC lines, and formats
it again with those entries in the contents. It repeats this until the
list stops changing, at most four times; usually two passes are
enough.
:h2.Where the files are
:fig.
:table cols='12 20'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.File
:c.What it is
:ethd.
:row.
:c.languages/bookie/bookie.py
:c.The translator and the formatting driver
:row.
:c.languages/bookie/vmplus.tmac
:c.The vmplus style
:row.
:c.languages/bookie/SAMPLE.SCRIPT
:c.A sample document (on the language disk as SAMPLE SCRIPT)
:row.
:c.linux390/vmspool/cms/BOOKIE.EXEC
:c.The CMS command
:row.
:c.linux390/vmspool/handlers/bookie
:c.The spool bridge service
:row.
:c./usr/local/lib/bookie
:c.bookie.py and vmplus.tmac in the Debian guest
:row.
:c.manual/mkbookie.js
:c.Makes GUIDE SCRIPT, the VM/370plus Guide in BookMaster markup,
from the same content as the Word edition
:row.
:c.manual/bookmaster
:c.This book and the Status book, as SCRIPT sources
:etable.
:figcap.bookie's files (paths in the project repository)
:efig.
:h1.Differences from BookMaster
:p.bookie covers the markup that manuals, memos and README-style books
use. These parts of BookMaster are different or missing:
:ul.
:li.:hp2.Not done:ehp2.: the index (&colon.i1. and its relatives),
lists of figures and tables, revision codes, profiles and document
options, &colon.artwork., and two-column layouts. Their tags are
either ignored or, if bookie does not know them, left in the text.
:li.:hp2.References:ehp2.: &colon.hdref. and &colon.figref. resolve
only to headings earlier in the source, and give the heading text,
not a page number. A forward reference prints its refid.
:li.:hp2.Highlighting:ehp2.: levels 4 to 9 repeat 1 to 3.
:li.:hp2.Footnotes:ehp2.: in parentheses in the vmplus style.
:li.:hp2.Headings:ehp2.: &colon.h4. to &colon.h6. look like
&colon.h3.; the contents list levels 1 and 2.
:li.:hp2.Imbeds on CMS:ehp2.: BOOKIE EXEC imbeds one level, with the
same file type. bookie.py on Linux goes 8 levels deep.
:li.:hp2.Control words:ehp2.: only the six in chapter 7.
:li.:hp2.Style ms:ehp2.: the contents come at the end, and there is no
running head.
:eul.
:p.Two limits come from CMS and the bridge, not from bookie: source
lines should stay within 72 columns (CMS files of 80), and the
formatting must finish within the bridge's limit of 900 seconds. A
15-page book takes under two minutes in the Debian guest; the 52-page
VM/370plus Guide takes several.
:appendix.
:h1.Tag Summary
:fig.
:table cols='12 6 18'.
:thd.
:row.
:c.Tag
:c.End tag
:c.Meaning
:ethd.
:row.
:c.&colon.userdoc.
:c.&colon.euserdoc.
:c.The document
:row.
:c.&colon.prolog.
:c.&colon.eprolog.
:c.Title-page fields: &colon.prodname. &colon.subtitle. &colon.title.
&colon.stitle. &colon.version. &colon.based. &colon.date.
&colon.author. &colon.docnum. &colon.filenum.
:row.
:c.&colon.frontm.
:c.
:c.Front matter
:row.
:c.&colon.tipage.
:c.
:c.The title page
:row.
:c.&colon.edition title='t'.
:c.&colon.eedition.
:c.The edition notice
:row.
:c.&colon.toc.
:c.
